1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
 If you trade, you need to hear this.

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:05,060
 Say you want to predict whether your bus is going to be late.

3
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:09,940
 So every day for the last three years, you stand at the bus stop and you count red cars.

4
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:10,980
 And you notice something.

5
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:17,040
 On the days you count more than 15 or more red cars, the bus is late 8 out of 10 times.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,120
 Congrats, you just did a bad test.

7
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:20,460
 You made a rule.

8
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,380
 15 or more red cars means the bus will be late.

9
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:29,180
 You checked it against three years of past warnings and it was right 8 out of 10 times.

10
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:30,780
 Trading works the same way.

11
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:33,860
 The red cars are your segment, the thing you watch.

12
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:36,580
 The late bus is the price curve you're betting on.

13
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:39,040
 In the last three years of counting, that is your data.

14
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:40,380
 But here's the real question.

15
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:43,080
 Does the red cars actually make your bus late?

16
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:43,740
 Of course not.

17
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:47,180
 More red cars just means more cars on the road, more traffic.

18
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:49,360
 And traffic is what makes the bus late.

19
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:52,480
 The red cars just happen to show up at the right time.

20
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:55,820
 That's called correlation versus causation.

21
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:59,600
 Two things happening together doesn't mean one causes the other.

22
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:02,840
 And it's one of the best ways bad tests fails you.

23
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,700
 Now what happens when something changes?

24
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,640
 The city adds a bus only lane.

25
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,160
 Now traffic doesn't slow the bus down anymore.

26
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:15,360
 So you count 15 red cars, you bet that the bus will be late, but it comes up right on time.

27
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:16,540
 Your rules just broke.

28
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:17,960
 Your counting wasn't wrong.

29
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,100
 It's just that your three years of data came from a street that doesn't exist anymore.

30
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:24,240
 In trading, that's called regime change.

31
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:27,340
 The secret dependency you're betting on disappears.

32
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:28,500
 And one more thing.

33
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:29,800
 Why 15 cars?

34
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:30,760
 Why not 12?

35
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:31,500
 Why not 20?

36
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:38,440
 If you tried every number from 5 to 30 and picked 15 because it got the best score, you didn't find a pattern.

37
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:41,860
 You found a number that fit what you wanted to hear.

38
00:01:41,980 --> 00:01:43,240
 That's called overfitting.

39
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:46,380
 So a good bad test doesn't just ask, did it work?

40
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,180
 It asks, why did it work?

41
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:49,740
 And is it still true today?

42
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,240
 Common Cube, and I'll send you the full breakdown.

